<h2>1. What Is Linux Patching?</h2>

<p>
Patching means installing updates to fix security issues, bugs and software problems in an operating system.
</p>

<p>Ubuntu uses the APT package manager to manage software updates.</p>

<p>Check available updates manually:</p>

<pre>sudo apt update</pre>

<p>Install available package updates:</p>

<pre>sudo apt upgrade -y</pre>

<p>
Ansible allows you to perform these operations on managed systems using a playbook instead of running commands manually.
</p>

<h2>2. Check Ansible Connectivity</h2>

<p>
Before patching, verify that Ansible can connect to the managed host.
</p>

<pre>
ansible ansible-client -m ansible.builtin.ping
</pre>

<p>
A successful response confirms that Ansible can communicate with the managed host.
</p>

<h2>3. Create Your First Patching Playbook</h2>

<p>Create a playbook:</p>

<pre>vi ubuntu-patching.yml</pre>

<p>Press <code>i</code> and add:</p>

<pre>
---
- name: Ubuntu System Patching
  hosts: ansible-client
  become: true

  tasks:
    - name: Update APT package cache
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        update_cache: true

    - name: Upgrade installed packages
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        upgrade: dist
</pre>

<p>Save the file:</p>

<pre>
Esc
:wq
</pre>

<p>
<strong>Explanation:</strong>
</p>

<ul>
<li><code>hosts</code> specifies the managed host.</li>
<li><code>become: true</code> runs tasks with elevated privileges.</li>
<li><code>ansible.builtin.apt</code> manages Ubuntu packages.</li>
<li><code>update_cache: true</code> refreshes the package information.</li>
<li><code>upgrade: dist</code> upgrades installed packages and handles dependency changes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Execute the Patching Playbook</h2>

<p>Check the playbook for syntax errors:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook --syntax-check ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<p>Execute the patching playbook:</p>

<pre>
ansible-playbook ubuntu-patching.yml
</pre>

<p>
Ansible refreshes the package cache and installs available updates on the managed host.
</p>

<h2>5. Check Available Updates</h2>

<p>
You can check whether updates are available before installing them.
</p>

<p>Add this task under the existing <code>tasks</code> section:</p>

<pre>
    - name: Check available package updates
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: apt list --upgradable
      register: available_updates
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display available updates
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: available_updates.stdout_lines
</pre>

<p>
The command lists packages that have newer versions available. The debug task displays the result.
</p>

<h2>6. Clean Unused Packages</h2>

<p>
After patching, you can remove packages that were installed as dependencies but are no longer required.
</p>

<p>Add this task to the playbook:</p>

<pre>
    - name: Remove unused packages
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        autoremove: true
</pre>

<p>
The <code>autoremove</code> option removes packages APT considers unnecessary.
</p>

<div class="warning">
Review package removal carefully before using autoremove on important systems.
</div>

<h2>7. Check Whether a Restart Is Required</h2>

<p>
Some updates require a system restart before they take full effect. Ubuntu can indicate this through the <code>/var/run/reboot-required</code> file.
</p>

<p>Add these tasks:</p>

<pre>
    - name: Check whether restart is required
      ansible.builtin.stat:
        path: /var/run/reboot-required
      register: reboot_status

    - name: Display restart status
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "A system restart is required."
      when: reboot_status.stat.exists

    - name: Display no-restart status
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "No system restart is required."
      when: not reboot_status.stat.exists
</pre>

<p>
The stat module checks whether the file exists. Ansible displays the appropriate message based on the result.
</p>

<div class="note">
This playbook only reports whether a restart is required. It does not restart the system automatically.
</div>

<h2>8. Real-Time Project: Ubuntu Patching Automation</h2>

<div class="project">
<strong>Project objective</strong>

<p>
Build a complete Ansible playbook that checks available updates, patches Ubuntu, removes unused packages and reports whether a system restart is required.
</p>
</div>

<h3>Step 1: Create the project playbook</h3>

<pre>vi ubuntu-maintenance.yml</pre>

<h3>Step 2: Add the complete playbook</h3>

<pre>
---
- name: Ubuntu Patching and Maintenance
  hosts: ansible-client
  become: true

  tasks:

    - name: Refresh APT package cache
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        update_cache: true
        cache_valid_time: 3600

    - name: Check available package updates
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: apt list --upgradable
      register: available_updates
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display available updates
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: available_updates.stdout_lines

    - name: Upgrade installed packages
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        upgrade: dist
      register: patch_result

    - name: Remove unused packages
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        autoremove: true

    - name: Clean downloaded package files
      ansible.builtin.apt:
        autoclean: true

    - name: Check whether restart is required
      ansible.builtin.stat:
        path: /var/run/reboot-required
      register: reboot_status

    - name: Display patching result
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Ubuntu patching completed."

    - name: Display restart requirement
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "A system restart is required."
      when: reboot_status.stat.exists

    - name: Display no-restart status
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "No system restart is required."
      when: not reboot_status.stat.exists
</pre>

<h3>Step 3: Validate the playbook</h3>

<pre>
ansible-playbook --syntax-check ubuntu-maintenance.yml
</pre>

<h3>Step 4: Execute the playbook</h3>

<pre>
ansible-playbook ubuntu-maintenance.yml
</pre>

<h3>Step 5: Verify installed package updates</h3>

<p>Check the package information:</p>

<pre>
ansible ansible-client -m ansible.builtin.command -a 'apt list --upgradable'
</pre>

<p>
If no updates are available, the output will indicate that no packages can be upgraded.
</p>

<h3>Step 6: Run the playbook again</h3>

<pre>
ansible-playbook ubuntu-maintenance.yml
</pre>

<p>
The second execution checks the system again. If packages are already up to date, there should be no additional package upgrades.
</p>

